Ladybird now places a tab from where its item starts on its line,
measured from the content box’s start edge past floats and the indent,
whether the line began at a soft wrap or a forced break. Chrome 158 and
Firefox 151 give identical results for every case of the regression test
(soft wraps at a space and at a wbr, two wraps, a forced break, a padded
span, a length tab-size, a first-line indent, a float on the first and
on a wrapped line, and vertical text). Blink’s LineBreaker shapes a tab
with ShapeResult::CreateForTabulationCharacters() at the line breaker’s
position_ plus ComputeFloatOffset(), and Gecko measures from the block’s
origin through nsTextFrame’s mOffsetFromBlockOriginForTabs.

WebKit 26.5 matches except beside a float, where it measures from
the line’s start past the float, and in vertical text. The engines
disagree on a right-to-left tab after a wrap (Chrome and WebKit put
the text after it 15px from the line’s left, Firefox 95px), so the
test leaves that case out.
